\title{Monochromatic Pythagorean Triples in Every Level Set of a
Completely Multiplicative Coloring}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-09}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We study Pythagorean triples $(x, y, z)$ with $x^2 + y^2 = z^2$ under
completely multiplicative colorings of the positive integers, that is,
maps $f : \mathbb{Z}_{>0} \to G$ into a finite abelian group $G$
satisfying $f(1) = 1$ and $f(mn) = f(m)f(n)$. A Pythagorean triple is
\emph{monochromatic of color $\omega$} when $f(x) = f(y) = f(z) = \omega$.
Our central result is a \emph{reduction}: the existence of a single
monochromatic Pythagorean triple of \emph{any} color forces, for every
color $\omega$ in the image of $f$, the existence of a Pythagorean triple
monochromatic of color exactly $\omega$. Consequently the realizability of
colors by monochromatic triples is an \emph{all-or-nothing} phenomenon
governed by the image subgroup of $f$. In particular, the special case
$\omega = 1$ (the neutral color) is logically equivalent to the full
every-color statement, even though the naive substitution $n \mapsto
f(n)\omega^{-1}$ fails to be multiplicative. The argument rests on two
structural facts: the scale invariance of Pythagorean triples, and the
fact that the image of a completely multiplicative map into a
\emph{finite} group is a subgroup. We isolate the genuinely analytic
content --- the existence of even one monochromatic triple --- as an
explicit hypothesis and derive several unconditional corollaries,
including that a monochromatic $(3,4,5)$ suffices to realize all colors.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

The interaction between multiplicative structure and additive Diophantine
configurations is a persistent theme in number theory. Pythagorean
triples --- integer solutions of $x^2 + y^2 = z^2$ --- are the archetypal
additive configuration, while completely multiplicative functions are the
archetypal carriers of multiplicative structure. A completely
multiplicative coloring assigns to each positive integer a ``color'' in a
finite abelian group $G$ in a way that respects multiplication:
\[
  f(1) = 1, \qquad f(mn) = f(m)f(n) \quad (m, n \ge 1).
\]
The prototypical example takes $G = \mu_k$, the group of $k$-th roots of
unity; then $f$ is a Dirichlet-character-like $k$-coloring of the
integers. However, nothing in our arguments uses more than the finite
abelian group structure, so we state everything at that level of
generality; $\mu_k$ is then a special case.

Given a color $\omega \in G$, its \emph{level set} (or \emph{color class})
is the fiber $f^{-1}(\{\omega\})$. We ask a Ramsey-type question: does
every level set of a completely multiplicative coloring contain a
Pythagorean triple? Equivalently, for each color $\omega$ appearing in
$f$, is there a Pythagorean triple $(x, y, z)$ with $f(x) = f(y) = f(z) =
\omega$?

At first sight the general color $\omega$ seems strictly harder than the
neutral color $\omega = 1$. Indeed the natural attempt to reduce a general
color to the neutral one --- replacing $f$ by $n \mapsto f(n)\omega^{-1}$
--- destroys multiplicativity, since $f(mn)\omega^{-1} \ne
(f(m)\omega^{-1})(f(n)\omega^{-1})$ in general. This paper shows that,
despite the failure of that naive substitution, the reduction \emph{does}
hold, by a different mechanism: one moves the \emph{triple}, not the
\emph{coloring}.

\section{Definitions and setup}

Throughout, $G$ is a finite abelian group written multiplicatively, with
identity $1$.

\begin{definition}[Completely multiplicative coloring]
A function $f : \mathbb{Z}_{\ge 0} \to G$ is a \emph{completely
multiplicative coloring} if $f(1) = 1$ and $f(mn) = f(m)f(n)$ for all
positive integers $m, n$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Pythagorean triple]
A triple $(x, y, z)$ of positive integers is a \emph{Pythagorean triple}
if $x, y, z > 0$ and $x^2 + y^2 = z^2$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Monochromatic triple]
A triple $(x, y, z)$ is \emph{monochromatic} under $f$ if $f(x) = f(y)$ and
$f(y) = f(z)$. It is \emph{monochromatic of color $\omega$} if in addition
$f(x) = f(y) = f(z) = \omega$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Image]
A color $g \in G$ is \emph{in the image} of $f$, written $g \in
\Image(f)$, if there exists a positive integer $n$ with $f(n) = g$.
\end{definition}

\section{Scale invariance}

\begin{lemma}[Scaling preserves Pythagorean triples]\label{lem:scale}
If $(x,y,z)$ is a Pythagorean triple and $t \ge 1$, then $(tx, ty, tz)$ is
a Pythagorean triple.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Positivity of $tx, ty, tz$ follows from positivity of the factors. For the
equation, $(tx)^2 + (ty)^2 = t^2(x^2 + y^2) = t^2 z^2 = (tz)^2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Colors under scaling]\label{lem:colorscale}
If $f$ is a completely multiplicative coloring and $t, n \ge 1$, then $f(tn)
= f(t)f(n)$. Hence a monochromatic triple of color $v$ becomes, after
scaling by $t$, a monochromatic triple of color $f(t)\,v$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Powers]\label{lem:pow}
If $f$ is a completely multiplicative coloring and $n \ge 1$, then $f(n^j)
= f(n)^j$ for every $j \ge 0$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Induction on $j$. The base case is $f(n^0) = f(1) = 1 = f(n)^0$. For the
step, $f(n^{j+1}) = f(n^j \cdot n) = f(n^j)f(n) = f(n)^j f(n) =
f(n)^{j+1}$.
\end{proof}

\section{The image is a subgroup}

\begin{lemma}[Identity]\label{lem:one}
The neutral color satisfies $1 \in \Image(f)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
$f(1) = 1$ with $1 > 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Closure under products]\label{lem:mul}
If $a, b \in \Image(f)$ then $ab \in \Image(f)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Write $a = f(m)$, $b = f(n)$ with $m, n > 0$. Then $ab = f(m)f(n) = f(mn)$
and $mn > 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Closure under inverses]\label{lem:inv}
If $g \in \Image(f)$ then $g^{-1} \in \Image(f)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Write $g = f(n)$ with $n > 0$. Since $G$ is finite of order $N = |G|$,
every element satisfies $g^N = 1$, hence $g^{N-1} \cdot g = 1$, so $g^{-1}
= g^{N-1}$. By Lemma~\ref{lem:pow}, $g^{N-1} = f(n)^{N-1} = f(n^{N-1})$
with $n^{N-1} > 0$. Therefore $g^{-1} \in \Image(f)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:subgroup}
$\Image(f)$ is a subgroup of $G$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Immediate from Lemmas~\ref{lem:one}--\ref{lem:inv}.
\end{proof}

The role of finiteness is essential and confined to Lemma~\ref{lem:inv}.
For a general infinite target group, the image of a completely
multiplicative map is only a submonoid.

\section{The reduction and the dichotomy}

\begin{theorem}[The Reduction]\label{thm:reduction}
Let $f$ be a completely multiplicative coloring into a finite abelian group
$G$. Suppose there exists a single monochromatic Pythagorean triple ---
that is, $(a, b, c)$ with $a,b,c$ a Pythagorean triple, $f(a) = f(b)$, and
$f(b) = f(c)$. Then for every color $\omega \in \Image(f)$ there exists a
Pythagorean triple $(x, y, z)$ with $f(x) = f(y) = f(z) = \omega$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $v_0 := f(a)$; by monochromaticity $f(a) = f(b) = f(c) = v_0$, and $v_0
\in \Image(f)$ since $a > 0$. Fix a target color $\omega \in \Image(f)$.
By Proposition~\ref{prop:subgroup}, the color gap $v_0^{-1}\omega$ lies in
$\Image(f)$: $v_0^{-1} \in \Image(f)$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:inv} and the
product with $\omega$ stays in the image by Lemma~\ref{lem:mul}. Choose $t
> 0$ with $f(t) = v_0^{-1}\omega$.

Set $(x, y, z) := (ta, tb, tc)$. By Lemma~\ref{lem:scale} this is a
Pythagorean triple. Its colors are
\[
  f(ta) = f(t)f(a) = (v_0^{-1}\omega)\,v_0 = \omega,
\]
using commutativity to cancel $v_0^{-1} v_0 = 1$; identically $f(tb) =
f(tc) = \omega$. Thus $(x,y,z)$ is monochromatic of color exactly $\omega$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[All-or-Nothing Dichotomy]\label{thm:dichotomy}
Let $f$ be a completely multiplicative coloring into a finite abelian
group. The following are equivalent:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item there exists a monochromatic Pythagorean triple of the neutral
    color $1$; and
  \item for every $\omega \in \Image(f)$ there exists a Pythagorean triple
    monochromatic of color $\omega$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$(1 \Rightarrow 2)$: A triple monochromatic of color $1$ is in particular
monochromatic, so Theorem~\ref{thm:reduction} applies. $(2 \Rightarrow
1)$: The neutral color $1$ lies in $\Image(f)$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:one}, so
instantiating (2) at $\omega = 1$ gives a monochromatic triple of color
$1$.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:dichotomy} refutes the intuition that the general-color
problem is strictly harder than the neutral case: the two are equivalent.
The genuine difficulty is not \emph{which} color, but whether \emph{any}
monochromatic triple exists.

\section{Corollaries and non-vacuity}

\begin{corollary}[The $(3,4,5)$ criterion]\label{cor:345}
If $f(3) = f(4) = f(5)$, then for every $\omega \in \Image(f)$ there is a
Pythagorean triple monochromatic of color $\omega$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
$(3,4,5)$ is a Pythagorean triple ($9 + 16 = 25$) that is monochromatic by
hypothesis; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:reduction}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Non-vacuity]\label{cor:nonvacuity}
The hypothesis of Theorem~\ref{thm:reduction} is satisfiable. For the
trivial coloring $f \equiv 1$, the triple $(3,4,5)$ is monochromatic, so
every color (here only $1$) is realized.
\end{corollary}

\section{Relationship to the analytic core}

The results above cleanly separate two layers. The
algebraic/combinatorial layer --- passing from one monochromatic triple to
all colors --- is fully settled by scale invariance and the subgroup
structure of the image. The analytic layer --- the existence of even one
monochromatic Pythagorean triple for an arbitrary completely multiplicative
coloring --- is the remaining deep input, isolated as the hypothesis of
Theorem~\ref{thm:reduction}. The motivating instance is $G = \mu_k$ with
$\omega = 1$, where an approximate-concentration argument forces three
multiplicative values on a Pythagorean configuration to coincide, exactly
because $\mu_k$ is discrete. Our reduction shows that establishing this one
existence statement --- for any single color --- immediately yields the
full every-color theorem.

\section{Algorithms}

The proofs are constructive.

\textbf{Algorithm A (Color transport).} Given a monochromatic seed triple
$(a, b, c)$ of color $v_0$, a coloring $f$, and a target $\omega \in
\Image(f)$: compute $\gamma := v_0^{-1}\omega$; find $t > 0$ with $f(t) =
\gamma$; return $(ta, tb, tc)$.

\textbf{Algorithm B (Inverse color as a power).} Given $g = f(n)$ and $N =
|G|$, return $n^{N-1}$; then $f(n^{N-1}) = g^{-1}$.

\textbf{Algorithm C (Spectrum enumeration).} Enumerate $\{f(n) : 1 \le n
\le M\}$; once one small monochromatic triple is found, Algorithm A
realizes the entire spectrum.

\section{Discussion and future work}

The reduction reorganizes the problem, converting a family of questions
indexed by colors into a single existence question, and exposing the coset
structure of admissible scale factors. The scaling engine is not special
to squares; any homogeneous, scale-invariant Diophantine relation inherits
the same all-or-nothing law. Future directions include: establishing the
existence of a single monochromatic triple for every completely
multiplicative coloring; quantifying the density of realizing scales;
constructing simultaneous disjoint monochromatic triples across all colors;
and extending the reduction to arbitrary homogeneous scale-invariant
Diophantine relations.

\section{Conclusion}

For completely multiplicative colorings into a finite abelian group, the
realizability of colors by monochromatic Pythagorean triples is governed
entirely by the image subgroup and is an all-or-nothing invariant. A single
monochromatic triple of any color propagates to every color via triple
scaling; the neutral-color case is equivalent to the full statement; and a
monochromatic $(3,4,5)$ suffices unconditionally.

